    "description": "The InventoryMessageAdapter and InventoryHttpImporter both convert external payloads into an InventoryItem. Their implementations duplicate the same ordered changes: trim text fields, parse numeric and date values, convert country codes to a canonical form, reject missing identifiers, and produce a domain value. The two entry points receive slightly different envelopes, but after envelope removal they must apply identical validation and normalization. Each change is pure, takes one value, and returns the next value; none may access the database, clock, or network. The team wants the order declared once, the individual changes to remain independently testable, and the resulting transformation to be passed to both adapters and reused by a scheduled CSV importer. A failed change should identify itself in tests without requiring either adapter to run. Avoid a shared mutable normalization object because adapters may execute concurrently.",
    "decision_statement": "How should the shared pure normalization sequence be represented so its order is declared once and the complete transformation can be reused across the three adapters?",
    "notes": "function-composition fits the pure unary normalization steps ordered once and reused as one transformation across three adapters; pipeline is the near miss for staged processing, with functional-core-imperative-shell supporting the pure-core/adapter-shell split."
  },
